1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,233
One sheet of graph paper.

2
00:00:02,233 --> 00:00:03,501
Six simple rules.

3
00:00:03,501 --> 00:00:05,572
Six completely different shapes.

4
00:00:05,972 --> 00:00:07,188
A slanted line.

5
00:00:07,188 --> 00:00:08,249
A flat line.

6
00:00:08,249 --> 00:00:09,149
AU.

7
00:00:08,836 --> 00:00:09,794
An S bend.

8
00:00:09,794 --> 00:00:10,694
AV.

9
00:00:10,382 --> 00:00:13,246
And two curves that never touch the axes.

10
00:00:13,696 --> 00:00:16,895
Aarav drew all six last
night at his study table.

11
00:00:17,245 --> 00:00:18,436
Here is the puzzle.

12
00:00:18,436 --> 00:00:21,676
Looking only at a shape, can
you tell which rule drew it?

13
00:00:22,176 --> 00:00:25,007
You can, once you see
how every graph is built.

14
00:00:25,657 --> 00:00:27,628
Every graph starts as a table.

15
00:00:27,978 --> 00:00:28,949
Pick an x.

16
00:00:28,949 --> 00:00:30,786
Use the rule to find y.

17
00:00:30,786 --> 00:00:32,018
Plot the point.

18
00:00:32,018 --> 00:00:33,626
Then join the points.

19
00:00:34,026 --> 00:00:38,841
Watch it for y equals x squared, with
x running from minus three to three.

20
00:00:39,241 --> 00:00:41,035
Minus three gives nine.

21
00:00:41,035 --> 00:00:42,720
Minus two gives four.

22
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,350
Minus one gives one.

23
00:00:44,350 --> 00:00:45,696
Zero gives zero.

24
00:00:46,096 --> 00:00:48,172
Then one, four and nine again.

25
00:00:48,172 --> 00:00:50,688
Join the dots, and a U shape appears.

26
00:00:50,688 --> 00:00:52,332
It is called a parabola.

27
00:00:52,982 --> 00:00:56,273
Two shapes from earlier in
the series are even simpler.

28
00:00:56,623 --> 00:01:01,546
The identity, y equals x, gives back
what you put in: a straight line through

29
00:01:01,546 --> 00:01:02,446
the origin.

30
00:01:02,834 --> 00:01:07,705
A constant function, like y equals
three, gives the same output every time:

31
00:01:07,705 --> 00:01:08,734
a flat line.

32
00:01:09,384 --> 00:01:11,543
Now watch the power of x grow.

33
00:01:11,543 --> 00:01:13,503
y equals x to the power n.

34
00:01:13,853 --> 00:01:16,484
At n equals one, it is the straight line.

35
00:01:16,884 --> 00:01:20,102
At n equals two, the
line bends into the U.

36
00:01:20,102 --> 00:01:22,874
A negative times a negative is positive.

37
00:01:23,274 --> 00:01:26,359
At n equals three, the
left arm flips down.

38
00:01:26,359 --> 00:01:28,552
Minus two cubed is minus eight.

39
00:01:28,552 --> 00:01:31,171
The cube keeps the sign: an S bend.

40
00:01:31,821 --> 00:01:32,893
Side by side.

41
00:01:32,893 --> 00:01:36,221
For x squared, minus two
and two both give four.

42
00:01:36,221 --> 00:01:39,756
It is a mirror image, and
it never goes below zero.

43
00:01:40,156 --> 00:01:42,912
For x cubed, minus two gives minus eight.

44
00:01:42,912 --> 00:01:45,821
The curve climbs from
far below to far above.

45
00:01:46,421 --> 00:01:47,541
Now you try.

46
00:01:47,541 --> 00:01:50,225
Can x squared ever equal minus four?

47
00:01:50,225 --> 00:01:51,616
Pause and decide.

48
00:01:54,816 --> 00:01:55,716
No!

49
00:01:55,301 --> 00:01:57,305
A square is never negative.

50
00:01:57,305 --> 00:02:00,078
So x squared has range zero and above.

51
00:02:00,078 --> 00:02:02,409
x cubed takes every real number.

52
00:02:03,059 --> 00:02:04,194
Remember this.

53
00:02:04,194 --> 00:02:08,743
Read the domain along the x axis,
and the range up the y axis.

54
00:02:09,443 --> 00:02:14,125
Shapes five and six came in the
last episode: one over x, and mod x.

55
00:02:14,125 --> 00:02:16,253
Now read them like a detective.

56
00:02:16,603 --> 00:02:19,219
Shine a light down onto the x axis.

57
00:02:19,219 --> 00:02:22,636
One over x casts a shadow
everywhere except zero.

58
00:02:22,636 --> 00:02:24,861
So its domain leaves out zero.

59
00:02:25,261 --> 00:02:27,840
Now shine it across onto the y axis.

60
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:32,561
The curve never reaches height zero,
so its range leaves out zero too.

61
00:02:32,961 --> 00:02:35,909
Mod x casts a shadow on the whole x axis.

62
00:02:35,909 --> 00:02:39,690
On the y axis, its shadow
starts at zero and goes up.

63
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:41,457
Side by side.

64
00:02:41,457 --> 00:02:45,055
The U of x squared and
the V of mod x look alike.

65
00:02:45,055 --> 00:02:49,469
Both pass through the origin, and
neither goes below the x axis.

66
00:02:49,869 --> 00:02:52,822
But the V has a sharp
corner and straight arms.

67
00:02:52,822 --> 00:02:55,396
The U is smooth and round at the bottom.

68
00:02:55,996 --> 00:02:56,896
Quick one.

69
00:02:56,861 --> 00:03:01,474
A graph passes through the origin,
never goes below the x axis, and has

70
00:03:01,474 --> 00:03:02,647
a sharp corner.

71
00:03:02,647 --> 00:03:03,671
Which is it?

72
00:03:03,671 --> 00:03:04,894
Pause and think.

73
00:03:08,094 --> 00:03:08,994
Mod x!

74
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,908
The sharp corner gives it away.

75
00:03:10,908 --> 00:03:13,215
The graph of x squared is smooth.

76
00:03:13,865 --> 00:03:15,601
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:15,951 --> 00:03:20,773
Where do the graphs of y equals x
squared and y equals mod x meet?

78
00:03:21,273 --> 00:03:22,187
Step one.

79
00:03:22,187 --> 00:03:24,908
For x zero or more, mod x is x.

80
00:03:24,908 --> 00:03:29,223
So x squared equals x, which
gives x equals zero or one.

81
00:03:29,973 --> 00:03:30,898
Step two.

82
00:03:30,898 --> 00:03:33,696
For negative x, mod x is minus x.

83
00:03:33,696 --> 00:03:38,287
So x squared equals minus x,
which gives x equals minus one.

84
00:03:39,037 --> 00:03:39,971
Step three.

85
00:03:39,971 --> 00:03:44,834
The meeting points are minus one comma
one, zero comma zero, and one comma

86
00:03:44,834 --> 00:03:45,734
one.

87
00:03:46,102 --> 00:03:48,514
The U and the V meet three times.

88
00:03:48,514 --> 00:03:50,955
The picture agrees with the algebra.

89
00:03:51,605 --> 00:03:53,071
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:03:53,421 --> 00:03:57,135
Find the range of x squared,
for x from minus one to two.

91
00:03:57,135 --> 00:04:00,354
Many students square the
ends and write one to four.

92
00:04:00,754 --> 00:04:05,912
But the U dips down to zero at x equals
zero, which is inside the interval.

93
00:04:05,912 --> 00:04:08,175
The true range is zero to four.

94
00:04:08,825 --> 00:04:10,328
Where is it asked?

95
00:04:10,328 --> 00:04:15,291
In JEE Main, in equations like x
squared minus mod x minus twelve equals

96
00:04:15,291 --> 00:04:17,334
zero: count the solutions.

97
00:04:17,934 --> 00:04:19,066
Exam level now.

98
00:04:19,066 --> 00:04:21,780
How many points do these two graphs share?

99
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:22,960
Pause and solve.

100
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:29,788
The answer is B.

101
00:04:29,788 --> 00:04:30,807
Two points.

102
00:04:31,307 --> 00:04:34,337
For x zero or more, x cubed equals x.

103
00:04:34,337 --> 00:04:36,118
So x is zero or one.

104
00:04:36,618 --> 00:04:39,441
For negative x, x cubed equals minus x.

105
00:04:39,441 --> 00:04:42,832
That needs x squared to
be minus one: impossible.

106
00:04:43,332 --> 00:04:46,888
On the left, the S bend goes
down while the V goes up.

107
00:04:46,888 --> 00:04:48,364
So, only two points.

108
00:04:48,964 --> 00:04:50,425
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:50,425 --> 00:04:51,926
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:55,126 --> 00:04:57,963
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:58,363 --> 00:04:59,263
One.

112
00:04:58,867 --> 00:05:02,116
Which of the six graphs
never touches either axis?

113
00:05:03,016 --> 00:05:03,916
Two.

114
00:05:03,514 --> 00:05:07,603
What is the range of x squared,
for x from minus two to one?

115
00:05:08,503 --> 00:05:09,403
Three.

116
00:05:09,227 --> 00:05:13,094
Where does y equals x
meet y equals one over x?

117
00:05:14,194 --> 00:05:18,314
Next time: a canteen bill adds
the cost of tea and a snack.

118
00:05:18,314 --> 00:05:20,131
Can we add two functions?

119
00:05:20,131 --> 00:05:22,343
Combining and comparing functions.
